course: dictionary
lesson: 11127
title: Whoopee: happy or sarcastic?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Whoopee!» is a shout you make when you are very happy and excited, like when your team wins or you get news you have hoped for. Listen to how it is said, because the feeling is in the voice as well as the word.
explain: The same word can mean the opposite. If someone says, «Oh, whoopee, more homework», in a flat, bored voice, they are being sarcastic: they mean that it is not exciting at all. The tone and the words around it are the clues.
explain: A whoopee cushion is a noun, not a shout. It is a joke rubber cushion that makes a rude noise when someone sits on it. Try your meaning in the sentence: «A whoopee cushion usually passes this test.» The joke cushion fits the sentence, so your meaning is probably right.
fact: Whoopee is an interjection: a word shouted out to show a feeling.
fact: «Whoopee!» means you are very happy or excited.
fact: Said in a flat, bored voice, «whoopee» means the opposite: «so what, that is not exciting».
fact: A whoopee cushion is a joke rubber cushion that makes a rude noise when someone sits on it.
fact: Tone of voice is a context clue: the same word can show joy or sarcasm.
exercise: «Whoopee! We won the match!» What does whoopee show? | A question about the score | Sadness and tiredness | *Great happiness and excitement | Anger at the referee
exercise: «Oh, whoopee, more homework», said in a flat voice. What does the speaker really mean? | They want to win a prize | *It is not exciting at all | They are going to sing | They are thrilled about the homework
exercise: What is a whoopee cushion? | A rubber toy for a bath | *A joke cushion that makes a rude noise when someone sits on it | A kind of bed for camping | A shout of joy
test: Why does tone of voice matter with «whoopee»? | Tone only matters in songs | *It can show joy or sarcasm, so the same word can mean the opposite | It changes the spelling of the word | It always shows the word is a noun
test: In «A whoopee cushion usually passes this test», what is a whoopee cushion? | *A joke cushion that makes a rude noise | A medal for winning a race | A kind of loud singing | A tent for a night out
exam: Which clue tells you that «Oh, whoopee, more homework» is sarcastic? | Lots of exclamation marks | The word Oh at the start | *A flat, bored tone of voice and the words «more homework» | It is written in a book
